-- Passage rg6-figurative-language-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('781cc872-cb16-529e-bd69-22390ad00008', 'rg6-figurative-language-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Kite on Harlan Hill$q$, $q$Every spring, the town of Millbrook held a kite festival on Harlan Hill. This year, Tomasz Novak had built his own kite from thin wooden rods and bright orange paper. He was proud of it, but he was also worried. His last kite had never left the ground.

On the morning of the festival, the wind was restless. It tugged at jackets, rattled the tent poles, and chased paper cups across the grass. Tomasz's friend Amara Osei held the kite while he backed away with the string.

"Now!" he called. Amara let go, and the kite leaped into the sky like a fish jumping from a stream. It climbed higher and higher until it was a tiny orange dot against the blue.

Then the wind changed its mind. The kite dipped and spun. Tomasz's stomach dropped with it. He pulled the string gently, the way his uncle had shown him, and the kite steadied itself.

All around them, other kites filled the sky. There were dragons, diamonds, and one enormous box kite that floated like a lazy cloud. The judges walked among the flyers, taking notes on clipboards.

When the prizes were announced, Tomasz did not win first place. He won the award for Best Homemade Kite. Amara thumped him on the back. "Your kite was the bravest one up there," she said.

Tomasz grinned. That evening, he could still feel the pull of the string in his hands, as if the sky had shaken hands with him.$q$, 'draft')
