  ('8f42cc55-b361-538c-8ce3-84bd8a78fdb3', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$In paragraph 6, Mei Lin says, "That was sneaky," then changes it to "I mean clever." Why does she change her word?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Sneaky\" and \"clever\" mean exactly the same thing, so the change does not matter.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"\"Clever\" is an insult, so she wants to tease Darnell after losing the game.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reverses-connotation"},{"text":"\"Sneaky\" can suggest cheating, while \"clever\" praises Darnell for smart thinking.","is_correct":true},{"text":"She believes Darnell broke a rule and wants the judges to hear her complaint.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Sneaky" can suggest cheating, while "clever" praises Darnell for smart thinking.$q$, $q$Both words describe a tricky move, but "sneaky" has a negative feeling and "clever" a positive one, and she laughs and shakes his hand. Nothing shows she thinks he broke a rule.$q$, 'draft', '8c671e9a-bac7-5fc2-8a86-4b9c1076c51a')
